      "0ca5af46-e3f2-4c09-82d7-75f92d5f99e4": {
        "children": [
          "7846b87d-09c0-4873-afe4-618734923f10"
        ],
        "id": "0ca5af46-e3f2-4c09-82d7-75f92d5f99e4",
        "message": {
          "author": {
            "metadata": {},
            "name": null,
            "role": "assistant"
          },
          "channel": null,
          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "Good progress \u2014 you\u2019ve locked in the **board + case + shelf**, and now it\u2019s just the \u201cdrive wobble\u201d and the HBA question. Let\u2019s go step by step.  \n\n---\n\n## 1. The 500 GB NVMe \u201ccache\u201d drive search  \nYou want **PCIe 3.0/4.0, TLC NAND, DRAM cache** (since L2ARC + ZIL benefit from DRAM for mapping).  \n\n### Kingston NV3 500 GB (SNV3S/500G, ~\u00a334)  \n- NAND: **QLC**, not TLC.  \n- DRAM: **DRAM-less** (uses Host Memory Buffer instead).  \n- Positioning: budget entry drive.  \n\ud83d\udc49 Not what you want for cache duties. Fine as a boot drive, but not as reliable/fast as TLC+DRAM for ZFS caching.  \n\n### Crucial P3 Plus 500 GB (CT500P3PSSD8, ~\u00a344)  \n- NAND: **QLC**.  \n- DRAM: **DRAM-less**.  \n- Positioning: cheap consumer SSD.  \n\ud83d\udc49 Again, not TLC+DRAM. Despite the \u201cPlus\u201d branding, it\u2019s QLC, so endurance and sustained write are weak.  \n\n### What to Look For  \n- Stick with **older models marketed as \u201cPro\u201d, \u201cEVO\u201d, or \u201cPlus (Gen 3)\u201d**. These tend to have TLC + DRAM.  \n- **Good candidates**:  \n  - Samsung 970 EVO Plus (PCIe 3.0, TLC + DRAM).  \n  - WD Black SN750 (PCIe 3.0, TLC + DRAM).  \n  - Crucial P5 Plus (Gen 4, TLC + DRAM).  \n  - Kingston KC2500 / KC3000 (TLC + DRAM).  \n\n\ud83d\udc49 These usually hover in the **\u00a340\u201355 range for 500 GB**, but stock levels are shaky.  \n\n---\n\n## 2. HBAs: Navigating the Alphabet Soup  \nZFS loves \u201cdumb\u201d HBAs in IT mode (no RAID logic, just pass-through). That\u2019s the target.  \n\n### Broadcom / LSI Families\n- **LSI 9211-8i**  \n  - PCIe 2.0 \u00d78, up to ~3.2 GB/s total.  \n  - SAS-2 (6 Gbps per lane).  \n  - Old, but still fine for spinning disks.  \n  - Often rebranded (IBM M1015, Dell H200, Fujitsu, etc).  \n\n- **LSI 9300-8i**  \n  - PCIe 3.0 \u00d78, up to ~6.4 GB/s.  \n  - SAS-3 (12 Gbps per lane).  \n  - More headroom for SSD-heavy setups.  \n  - Solid choice for modern builds.  \n\n- **LSI 9305-16i**  \n  - PCIe 3.0 \u00d78.  \n  - SAS-3, but with **16 ports on one card**.  \n  - Ideal for chassis with 12\u201316 drives, but pricey.  \n\n---\n\n### Variants You Named\n- **9201, 9223, 9240, 9272, etc.** \u2192 These are older SAS-2 RAID/HBA cards.  \n  - 9240 = usually RAID firmware, needs flashing to IT mode.  \n  - 9211 = the \u201cclassic\u201d HBA for ZFS, easily flashable to IT mode.  \n- **9300 series** \u2192 Newer, usually IT mode already available. Better for future-proofing.  \n- **9305** \u2192 Just the 9300 with more ports (16 instead of 8).  \n\n---\n\n### Practical Recommendation\n- For **6\u20138 spinning disks**, **LSI 9211-8i (PCIe 2.0) is plenty** \u2014 even full tilt, you\u2019ll never saturate PCIe 2.0 \u00d78 with HDDs.  \n- If you want **modern support and possible SSD pools later**, get a **9300-8i**.  \n- Don\u2019t overpay for a 9305 unless you know you\u2019ll need 12\u201316 drives.  \n\n\u26a0\ufe0f Watch out:  \n- Many eBay HBAs are **rebrands** (Dell Perc H310, IBM M1015, etc) \u2192 perfectly fine once flashed to LSI IT firmware.  \n- Some are **fakes** (poor-quality clones, especially from China). Look for listings with genuine Broadcom/LSI markings, or well-regarded sellers.  \n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Summary\n- **500 GB NVMe cache drive**: skip Kingston NV3 & Crucial P3+ (both QLC/DRAM-less). Look for Samsung 970 EVO Plus, WD SN750, Crucial P5+, Kingston KC2500.  \n- **HBA**:  \n  - Cheapest solid option \u2192 LSI 9211-8i (PCIe 2.0, SAS-2).  \n  - More modern/future-proof \u2192 LSI 9300-8i (PCIe 3.0, SAS-3).  \n  - Ignore 9305 unless you expect 12+ drives.  \n\n---\n\n\ud83d\udc49 Do you want me to do a **quick eBay UK scan for real HBAs (9211/9300)** that are likely genuine, so you\u2019ve got safe purchase links instead of wading through the clone jungle?"
            ]
          },
