              "Excellent \u2014 this is a really healthy-looking test run so far.  \nLet\u2019s go through each of your questions step by step:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\udde9 Why `sdb` failed to start\n\n> `/dev/sdb is apparently in use by the system; it's not safe to run badblocks!`\n\nThat means **the OS has a partition from `sdb` mounted or in use by LVM**.  \nIn your earlier `lsblk` output you had:\n\n```\nsdb \u251c\u2500sdb1  \n    \u251c\u2500sdb2  \n    \u2514\u2500sdb3  \n      \u2514\u2500ubuntu--vg-ubuntu--lv\n```\n\nThat last line means it\u2019s **part of an LVM volume group** (`ubuntu--vg`).  \nEven if nothing is actively mounted, **the kernel still thinks the LV is in use**, so `badblocks` refuses to write to it.\n\n\u2705 **Fix:**  \nRun these before the test:\n```bash\numount -A /dev/sdb*\nvgchange -an ubuntu--vg\n```\nThen re-run `badblocks -wsv /dev/sdb`.  \nThat safely detaches all logical and physical mappings.\n\nIf you don\u2019t care about the data (you\u2019re overwriting it anyway), this is 100% safe.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcc8 Understanding your `iostat` output\n\nLet\u2019s interpret your sample:\n\n```\navg-cpu:  %user   %nice %system %iowait  %steal   %idle\n           0.03    0.00    0.99   23.86    0.00   75.12\n```\n\n- **%iowait = 23.86%** \u2192 A quarter of CPU time is waiting for disks. That\u2019s normal during `badblocks -wsv`.\n- **%idle = 75%** \u2192 Plenty of CPU headroom \u2014 system is *not* overloaded.\n- So, healthy.\n\nThen your device lines:\n\n```\nDevice: sda ...  wMB/s 0.29\n```\n\nThat means sda is writing ~0.29 MB/s per sample. The low number is because `badblocks` writes one small pattern across all blocks, verifying each \u2014 not streaming I/O \u2014 so instantaneous throughput is deceptive. Expect ~60\u201380 MB/s average once it\u2019s running fully (on 500 GB HDDs, ~2\u20133 h total).\n\n**Interpretation:**\n| Column | Meaning |\n|:--|:--|\n| `r/s`, `w/s` | Reads/writes per second |\n| `rMB/s`, `wMB/s` | Read/write throughput in MB/s |\n| `%util` | How busy that disk is (should be <100% = healthy) |\n| `r_await`, `w_await` | Average latency in ms (under ~10ms for HDDs is fine, though can rise under badblocks) |\n\nAll your drives show around **7% util**, which fits early stages of a sequential test.  \nNo `nvme0n1` I/O \u2192 confirms nothing else is interfering.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf21\ufe0f Temperatures\n\nEverything < 40 \u00b0C is perfect. HDDs usually prefer 30\u201345 \u00b0C range.  \nYour readings (25\u201335 \u00b0C) are ideal \u2014 fans doing their job.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\udde0 About `nohup`, `2>&1`, and background `&`\n\nLet\u2019s clarify the mechanics:\n\n| Component | Function |\n|:--|:--|\n| `nohup` | Keeps the command running even after logout |\n| `2>&1` | Redirects STDERR (2) into STDOUT (1) so both go into the same log |\n| `&` | Backgrounds the process (releases terminal prompt) |\n\nSo together:\n```bash\nnohup ./script.sh > logfile 2>&1 &\n```\n= run detached, output unified, safe to close SSH.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddee Next step: write test\nYou can go straight to the **write-mode badblocks** test on `sda`, `sdc`, and `sdd` now.  \nSkip `sdb` until you deactivate LVM (`vgchange -an ubuntu--vg`).\n\n---\n\n### \u2699\ufe0f Quick verification before re-running `sdb`\nAfter shutting LVM:\n```bash\nlsblk\nlsof | grep sdb\nmount | grep sdb\n```\nAll should return **nothing** (i.e., disk not in use).  \nThen it\u2019s safe to re-run the write test.\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to modify your `parallel_bad.sh` script so it *automatically skips any drive that\u2019s mounted or part of an LVM* and logs a warning instead of aborting the whole run?  \nThat makes it resilient for future multi-disk tests."
