              "Perfect diagnostic work \u2014 you\u2019re reading the data exactly right, and yes:  \nthat slow speed on `sdc` and `sdd` is absolutely because of **both the SATA revision** *and* possibly **port contention** on the same controller channel.  \n\nLet\u2019s break down what\u2019s happening and how to classify these drives for reuse:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\udde9 Why `sdc` and `sdd` are crawling\n- Both are **Seagate Pipeline 5900 rpm** (a low-power AV/\u201cset-top box\u201d drive line).  \n  Their real-world sustained throughput is only **70\u2013100 MB/s**, *even on SATA 3*.\n- One of them (`sdc`) is **negotiating only 1.5 Gb/s (SATA I)** \u2014 that\u2019s **~120 MB/s max bus speed**.\n- The other (`sdd`) is at **3.0 Gb/s (SATA II)** \u2014 but if they\u2019re on the same controller lane, they share bandwidth and interrupt time.\n\nSo what you\u2019re seeing \u2014 17 hours and counting \u2014 is completely consistent with two slow 5900 rpm disks, one of them on SATA I, being hammered in parallel with 8\u00d7 full-surface read/write passes.\n\n---\n\n### \u2699\ufe0f How to classify the drives\n\n| Drive | Interface | Type | Realistic Role | Notes |\n|-------|------------|------|----------------|-------|\n| **sda \u2013 Samsung HD502HI** | ATA/ATAPI-7 \u2192 SATA I (1.5 Gb/s) | 5400 rpm desktop | \u2705 *Good for cold/offline backup* | Old, but healthy. Slow \u2014 don\u2019t use in live RAID. |\n| **sdb \u2013 Seagate ST500DM002** | SATA 3 (6 Gb/s) | 7200 rpm desktop | \ud83d\udfe2 *Primary candidate for RAID / production* | Modern, strong SMART data. |\n| **sdc \u2013 ST3500312CS** | SATA I (1.5 Gb/s) | 5900 rpm AV | \u26a0\ufe0f *Use as spare only* | Extremely slow; won\u2019t fail outright, but will bottleneck any array. |\n| **sdd \u2013 ST3500312CS** | SATA II (3 Gb/s) | 5900 rpm AV | \u26a0\ufe0f *Use as hot spare / temporary* | Slightly faster twin of `sdc`; still slow for RAID. |\n\nThat\u2019s exactly your instinct:  \n\u2705 keep `sdb` for real use  \n\ud83d\udfe0 keep `sda` as an offline backup  \n\u26a0\ufe0f relegate `sdc`/`sdd` to hot-spare or temporary testing roles.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddee SATA 1 vs 2 vs 3 in mixed testing\nYou asked: *Can I mix SATA 2.6 (3 Gb/s) and SATA 3.0 (6 Gb/s)?*\n\nYes \u2014 absolutely. SATA is fully backward compatible.  \nThe *controller* negotiates the highest common speed per port.  \nYou can have SATA 3 drives on some ports and SATA 1/2 on others without conflict.\n\nThe only caveat:\n- If you test **several drives on the same controller chip**, the slowest ones can hog command queue time and reduce throughput for others.\n- So when benchmarking an **ASM1166**, test ports in **pairs or triples** if you don\u2019t have six equal drives.\n- Using a **known-good SSD** (even a small one) is *very* helpful \u2014 it lets you see the controller\u2019s true bus speed independent of the drive.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd0d Confirming badblocks progress now\nSince your logs show progress at 17 hours and `badblocks` processes are still alive, let them continue:\n```bash\nwatch -n 300 \"grep 'done,' /root/disk_logs/badblocks_write_*.log | tail -10\"\n```\nThat updates every 5 min and shows movement percentages.\n\nOnce they finish, check:\n```bash\ngrep \"Pass completed\" /root/disk_logs/badblocks_write_*.log\n```\nand then rerun:\n```bash\nsmartctl -a /dev/sdX | grep -E \"Reallocated|Pending|Offline\"\n```\nIf any of those numbers > 0, the drive physically reallocated sectors during the test \u2014 meaning it\u2019s degraded, even if badblocks shows \u201c0 errors\u201d.\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to give you a **minimal test plan** for the ASM1166 (covering both SATA-3 SSDs and your slower HDDs) so you can confirm each port\u2019s real throughput without killing days per run?"
