              "Perfect \u2014 you\u2019re reading the output exactly right \ud83d\udc4d  \nLet\u2019s unpack what\u2019s happening and then I\u2019ll show you how to upgrade your script to do **parallel**, **long**, and **conditional** tests safely.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83e\udde9 Why it\u2019s repeating `/dev/sda`\nYou\u2019re right \u2014 that happens because each time you started and stopped the script, it began again from the top of the device list.  \nSince your `nohup` runs survive until you stop them manually, the logs you\u2019re seeing are just the repeated short test on `/dev/sda` (the first drive in the loop).\n\nYou can verify by checking how many scripts are still active:\n```bash\nps aux | grep disk_test.sh\n```\nIf you see several, kill them:\n```bash\nkillall disk_test.sh\n```\nThen start fresh.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83e\uddea 1. Short vs. Long SMART tests\n### Short test\n- Takes 1\u20133 minutes.  \n- Tests basic electronics and a bit of the surface.  \n- Usually enough for detecting *obvious* bad drives.\n\n### Long (Extended) test\n- Takes tens of minutes to hours (depends on drive size).  \n- Scans the *entire surface* and verifies every sector.  \n- The only reason **not** to do it is time.\n\nIf a short test fails \u2192 there\u2019s **no point** in doing a long test, because the drive already proved unreliable.\n\n---\n\n## \u2699\ufe0f 2. Running tests **in parallel**\nYou can safely run SMART tests on multiple drives at once \u2014 they operate independently.\n\nIf your script currently does something like:\n```bash\nfor DEV in /dev/sd[a-d]; do\n  smartctl -t short \"$DEV\"\n  sleep 120\ndone\n```\nIt\u2019s running them **sequentially**.  \nYou can make it **parallel** by backgrounding each test:\n\n```bash\nfor DEV in /dev/sd[a-d]; do\n  echo \"Starting SMART short test on $DEV\"\n  smartctl -t short \"$DEV\" > \"logs/${DEV##*/}_short.log\" 2>&1 &\ndone\nwait\necho \"All short tests started; waiting for them to complete...\"\nsleep 180\n```\n\n---\n\n## \ud83e\uddf1 3. Doing *long* tests automatically\nYou can extend the script like this:\n\n```bash\n#!/bin/bash\nLOGDIR=\"/root/disk_logs\"\nmkdir -p \"$LOGDIR\"\n\nfor DEV in /dev/sd[a-d]; do\n    MODEL=$(smartctl -i \"$DEV\" | awk -F: '/Device Model|Model Number/ {print $2}' | xargs)\n    SERIAL=$(smartctl -i \"$DEV\" | awk -F: '/Serial Number/ {print $2}' | xargs)\n    LOG=\"$LOGDIR/${DEV##*/}_${SERIAL}_$(date +%F_%H-%M).log\"\n\n    echo \"=== Testing $DEV ($MODEL, SN=$SERIAL) ===\" | tee -a \"$LOG\"\n    smartctl -t short \"$DEV\" >> \"$LOG\" 2>&1 &\ndone\n\nwait\necho \"Short tests launched \u2014 sleeping 3m\"\nsleep 180\n\n# check results and trigger long tests only if short passed\nfor DEV in /dev/sd[a-d]; do\n    STATUS=$(smartctl -l selftest \"$DEV\" | grep -m1 \"Completed without error\")\n    if [[ -n \"$STATUS\" ]]; then\n        echo \"$DEV short test passed \u2014 starting long test\"\n        smartctl -t long \"$DEV\" &\n    else\n        echo \"$DEV short test failed \u2014 skipping long test\"\n    fi\ndone\n\nwait\necho \"All long tests running; check with smartctl -a /dev/sdX\"\n```\n\nThat script:\n- Runs all short tests in **parallel**.\n- Waits a few minutes.\n- Starts long tests **only** on drives that passed short tests.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udca1 4. During and after the tests\nYou can monitor progress with:\n```bash\nsmartctl -a /dev/sda | grep -A3 \"Self-test\"\n```\nor view the most recent entry:\n```bash\nsmartctl -l selftest /dev/sda\n```\n\n---\n\n## \ud83e\uddfe Summary\n\n| Task | Sequential | Parallel | Notes |\n|------|-------------|-----------|-------|\n| Short test | \u2705 current script | \u2705 possible | Fast, detects obvious faults |\n| Long test | \u274c not yet | \u2705 recommended | Deeper surface check |\n| Fail on short | Skip long | Skip long | Saves time |\n| Logging | \u2705 yes | \u2705 with separate log files | Store by serial number |\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to rewrite your current `/root/disk_test.sh` to a **drop-in replacement** version that:\n- does both short and long tests,  \n- runs in parallel,  \n- and logs per-disk with serial number in filenames?"
